<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul Gauguin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Gauguin was a French Post-Impressionist painter, sculptor and printmaker. Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1891, he spent most of his remaining years in Tahiti and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a>, where he painted the works, bold in colour and simplified in form, that shaped European images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. He died at Atuona on Hiva Oa, and his work influenced modern artists from the Fauves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin.jpg" alt="Paul Gauguin" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis-Maurice Boutet de Monvel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Gauguin_1891.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gauguin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 7 June 1848, the son of Clovis Gauguin, a liberal journalist who had to flee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> when his newspaper was suppressed, and Aline Chazal, daughter of the engraver André Chazal and of the author and early socialist activist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flora-tristan">Flora Tristan</a>. In 1850 the family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, where he had a privileged childhood until the age of six. After schooling in France he signed on as a pilot's assistant in the merchant marine and then served two years in the French navy. In 1873 he married the Dane Mette-Sophie Gad, and they had five children.</p><p>He worked as a stockbroker and began painting in his spare time, befriending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Camille Pissarro</a>, who introduced him to other Impressionist artists. The financial crisis of 1882 damaged his brokerage career and led him to paint full time. He showed 19 paintings and a wood relief at the eighth and last Impressionist exhibition in May 1886.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under the influence of folk art and Japanese prints, his work moved towards Cloisonnism, with flat areas of colour and heavy black outlines, as in The Yellow Christ of 1889, and later towards Synthetism. In 1887 he travelled with the painter Charles Laval to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, where he worked as a labourer on the French canal, and then to Martinique. Vincent van Gogh and his brother Theo, a dealer with the firm Goupil &amp; Cie, admired his Martinique paintings, and Theo bought three of them for 900 francs.</p><p>After a successful auction at the Hôtel Drouot in February 1891, Gauguin sailed for Tahiti on 1 April, stating his aim of escaping European civilisation and everything artificial and conventional. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in August 1893 and left for Tahiti again on 28 June 1895.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a> he found a society that, as in Tahiti, had lost much of its cultural identity; the population had fallen from some 80,000 in the 18th century to 4,000, largely through Western diseases. In 1903 he campaigned against the island's gendarmes and on 27 March was fined 500 francs and sentenced to three months' imprisonment for libel. Weak, in great pain and using morphine, he died suddenly on 8 May 1903.</p><p>Retrospective exhibitions at the Salon d'Automne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1903 and 1906 strongly influenced the French avant-garde, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a>, whose work of 1906 led to Les Demoiselles d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> in 1907. In the 21st century critics have described his Primitivist depictions of Tahiti as colonialist and have pointed to his sexual exploitation of Tahitian girls.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul Gauguin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the 1880s younger painters, Vincent van Gogh, <b>Paul Gauguin</b>, Georges Seurat and Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec, reacted against the Impressionists’ concern with reproducing the optical sensations of light and colour and turned to symbolic content and the expression of emotion.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>After a quarrel with <b>Paul Gauguin</b> in Arles, Vincent van Gogh cut off part of his left ear.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The painter <b>Paul Gauguin</b> made its image famous in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-establishments-in-oceania">French Establishments in Oceania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Atuona. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1883" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paul Gauguin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1883">What was here in 1883? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
